WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.731
Return what helps the next step, including errors.

2
00:00:03.011 --> 00:00:08.856
What a tool returns lands in the model’s context, so it costs tokens and steers the next decision.

3
00:00:09.136 --> 00:00:21.317
The engineering post recommends returning high-signal fields and natural-language identifiers rather than opaque internal ones: name and file_type instead of uuid and mime_type.

4
00:00:21.597 --> 00:00:28.701
It reports that resolving cryptic IDs into meaningful language improved Claude’s precision in retrieval tasks.

5
00:00:28.981 --> 00:00:42.827
For tools that can return a lot, use pagination, filtering, or truncation with sensible defaults; the post mentions that Claude Code caps tool responses at 25,000 tokens by default.

6
00:00:43.107 --> 00:00:50.275
One option it describes is a response_format parameter that lets the agent ask for concise or detailed output.

7
00:00:50.555 --> 00:00:52.197
Errors deserve the same care.

8
00:00:52.477 --> 00:00:55.805
A traceback or “error 400” tells the model nothing.

9
00:00:56.085 --> 00:01:05.792
The handle-tool-calls docs suggest saying what went wrong and what to try next, and you saw why in lesson 10: the model reads the error and adjusts its next call.

10
00:01:06.072 --> 00:01:11.043
For the meeting-notes tool, compare “Invalid input” with “No teammate matches ‘Jordan’.

11
00:01:11.323 --> 00:01:14.907
Known teammates: Priya Raman, Marcus Lee, Dana Whitfield.

12
00:01:15.187 --> 00:01:18.579
If the person is not on the team, leave the owner unassigned.

13
00:01:18.859 --> 00:01:28.907
” The second turns a dead end into a decision rule, and it encodes your product policy (unknown owners stay unassigned) where the model will see it at the moment it matters.
